Chapter: BuiltinAsValue
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A builtin is emitted where it is called, so there is no function object to
 take the address of. Handing one to a parameter therefore cannot work, and
 until this was caught it did not fail -- it COMPILED, and the program died
 at runtime with !EXC=06 and no diagnostic anywhere.

 The mechanism was a check that had lost its teeth. An unresolved func-addr
 reference is patched to __unresolved_trap, whose body is the ud2 that
 raised the !EXC=06; check-func-addr-targets was supposed to refuse that,
 but it asked for `off == -1 & trap == -1` and the trap is always emitted,
 so the condition never held. The call-patch check beside it has no such
 clause and always refused an unresolved call. One extra conjunct was the
 whole difference between a diagnostic and a crash.

 Nothing here is about effect rows: the parameter's row is exactly
 print-line-uni's own, so the rows agree and the program gets all the way
 to codegen. That is deliberate -- an earlier row mismatch used to mask this
 by refusing the program for an unrelated reason.

Section: Body

  apply-it : (Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing), Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing
  apply-it (f) (t) = f t

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    apply-it print-line-uni "builtin"
  end
